.TH CBMAP_GET_PTR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_get_ptr \- get pointer to value by key (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

value_ptr = cbmap_get_ptr(hm_name, key);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for cbmap_get_elem_ref() that returns a pointer to
the value, or NULL if the key is not in the map. Note that cbmap_get()
stops the program in that case, while this macro does not.

The lookup key goes through the DECLARED key type of the tree, in the
same way as in cbmap_insert(3), so a key that the tree stored through
that conversion is only ever found again by a lookup that makes the same
one. A literal and a computed expression are both accepted.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  BST map to search
key      Key to look up
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the value if the key is in the map
Returns NULL if the key is not in the map
Returns NULL if key is a NULL character pointer, which is not a string
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program if the size of the value does not match the
size of the type
The macro returns a pointer to the value, so the caller can change the
value in place
An insert or a delete makes the pointer invalid
For a char* value type, the target of the pointer is const-qualified
(char *const *): the caller can read the stored string and edit the bytes
of that string in place, within the stored length, but a replacement of
the pointer itself is a compile error. The map owns the string bytes and
keeps its own accessor in step with them, and a replacement of the
pointer would leave the length of the accessor describing the older
string. To replace a string value, use cbmap_insert(3), which frees the
old bytes and copies the new bytes into storage that the map owns.
The search takes O(log n) time
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_construct(map, int, int);
int key = 42, val = 100;
cbmap_insert(map, key, val);
int* ptr = cbmap_get_ptr(map, key);
if (ptr) {
  *ptr = 200; // Change the value in place
}

cbmap_construct(names, int, char *);
cbmap_insert(names, key, "hello");
char *const *sptr = cbmap_get_ptr(names, key);
if (sptr) {
  printf("%s\en", *sptr);  // Read the stored string
  (*sptr)[0] = 'j';         // Edit its bytes in place: "jello"
  cbmap_insert(names, key, "a longer replacement");  // Replace the string
}
cbmap_destroy(map);
cbmap_destroy(names);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_get (3),
.BR cbmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR cbmap_insert (3)
